# Target C — C82 prescribed chain-atom Mantel frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_chain_atom_weighted_mantel_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_chain_atom_weighted_mantel_2026-08-31.md).

C80 forces `D` train tracks into one exact projective direction in the pure-rhombic
chain-face branch. Thus the limiting train-direction measure has an atom of mass at
least

`D/T=1/x`, where `x=T/D`.

Target B Section 39 gives total atomic mass

`s<=1-y/2`, `y=q/T`,

and positive-density rhombus crossings are supported on a triangle-free weighted graph
on those atoms.

A weighted-Mantel refinement with one prescribed atom gives

`x >= (1+Delta_2)/(1-y/2)+o(1)`,

where

`Delta_2=pi/(2sqrt(3))`.

Consequently an exact C80 chain face in the pure-rhombic asymptotic branch forces

`liminf T/D >= 2.5425329094...`,

`limsup q/T <= 0.9033545903...`,

and

`liminf (T-q)/D >= 0.3361033553...`.

The last number also improves Target B Section 40's lower bound on the dimension of
the first-order-neutral train-angle space.

## Effect

The exact chain face increases the higher-order blocker burden rather than resolving
it. The improvement is small because a triangle-free weighted crossing graph can put
the chain atom on one side of an almost balanced bipartition.

## Next target

The remaining cross-route options are now sharply separated:

1. upgrade C66 star-center beta concentration to one-edge concentration and invoke C81;
2. exploit interval structure of diameter-gradient restrictions on the ordered `D`
   chain tracks to beat the raw `q` rank count; or
3. combine the C80 negative-variance operator with Target B's backward/tangent blocker
   map and the limited non-hull terminal-train resource.